WEBVTT

NOTE SE201 Lesson 1: Four eras of building systems — captions generated from the narration script

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.384
Software engineering 201. From loop, to enterprise systems.

2
00:00:05.384 --> 00:00:08.860
Lesson one. Four eras of building systems.

3
00:00:09.210 --> 00:00:15.297
Quick question. An agent can now write a working piece of a billing system in an afternoon.

4
00:00:15.297 --> 00:00:17.972
So in a big system today, what's scarce?

5
00:00:17.972 --> 00:00:21.651
Code? Servers? Or knowing that what got built is right?

6
00:00:21.651 --> 00:00:23.992
Knowing it's right. Code got cheap.

7
00:00:23.992 --> 00:00:28.540
Checking it didn't. Thirty years ago, the answer was very different.

8
00:00:28.880 --> 00:00:31.958
I've been building systems for about thirty years.

9
00:00:31.958 --> 00:00:39.100
Systems built around money: electronic rent payments, a card-issuing platform, an investment adviser for nonprofits.

10
00:00:39.100 --> 00:00:45.442
And systems that sell: retail e-commerce and EDI, subscription billing, and today, e-commerce again.

11
00:00:45.442 --> 00:00:48.520
And the way you build a big system keeps changing.

12
00:00:48.870 --> 00:00:51.151
Here's a useful way to see why.

13
00:00:51.151 --> 00:00:54.610
Architecture bends around whatever is scarcest.

14
00:00:54.610 --> 00:00:58.951
Whatever is expensive, or slow, the design works around it.

15
00:00:58.951 --> 00:01:02.410
So for each of four eras, ask: what was scarce?

16
00:01:02.760 --> 00:01:06.048
Thirty years ago, the commercial web was brand new.

17
00:01:06.048 --> 00:01:11.076
You bought servers and racked them yourself, and more capacity was weeks away.

18
00:01:11.076 --> 00:01:17.136
So, typically, you built one big program, on one big database, and shipped a few times a year.

19
00:01:17.136 --> 00:01:23.196
Companies traded orders as overnight batch files, through EDI, and much of that still runs.

20
00:01:23.196 --> 00:01:25.130
What was scarce? The machines.

21
00:01:25.490 --> 00:01:28.683
Twenty years ago. The web was the front door.

22
00:01:28.683 --> 00:01:33.011
Browser, application, database: three tiers, on rented racks.

23
00:01:33.011 --> 00:01:37.126
And the first big public cloud services had just launched.

24
00:01:37.126 --> 00:01:39.680
Agile teams shipped every few weeks.

25
00:01:39.680 --> 00:01:41.809
What was scarce? Coordination.

26
00:01:41.809 --> 00:01:46.350
Getting a growing team to change one codebase without colliding.

27
00:01:46.700 --> 00:01:50.324
Ten years ago. The cloud, by default, for new systems.

28
00:01:50.324 --> 00:01:54.150
Many small services, in containers, owned by small teams.

29
00:01:54.150 --> 00:01:56.902
The best teams deployed many times a day.

30
00:01:56.902 --> 00:02:02.607
And parts they once built themselves became a service, behind an API. What was scarce?

31
00:02:02.607 --> 00:02:09.990
Seeing the whole. No one person could hold it all in their head, so we built dashboards and tracing to see it.

32
00:02:10.340 --> 00:02:13.564
And now. Agents can write much of the code.

33
00:02:13.564 --> 00:02:16.263
Building a piece is fast, and cheap.

34
00:02:16.263 --> 00:02:19.412
So what's scarce? Knowing that it's right.

35
00:02:19.412 --> 00:02:25.710
That, and context: how much of the system an agent can see, and understand, at once.

36
00:02:26.050 --> 00:02:32.300
Now a made-up example: a subscription-billing system, built today, the kind of platform I ran as CTO

37
00:02:32.300 --> 00:02:36.852
at sticky dot I O. Clear modules, with boundaries an agent can work inside.

38
00:02:36.852 --> 00:02:39.461
One repository, where it can find anything.

39
00:02:39.461 --> 00:02:42.131
Tests and specs it can check itself against.

40
00:02:42.131 --> 00:02:46.258
And decisions written down, because the agent wasn't in the meeting.

41
00:02:46.258 --> 00:02:51.780
My own team keeps its code that way: one repository, with instruction files for the agents.

42
00:02:52.140 --> 00:02:53.988
I'll be straight about that list.

43
00:02:53.988 --> 00:02:57.181
It's where the thinking is heading, not a settled answer.

44
00:02:57.181 --> 00:02:59.869
The evidence for it is still thin, and indirect.

45
00:02:59.869 --> 00:03:02.950
Every era had a confident answer, then found its costs.

46
00:03:03.290 --> 00:03:05.862
And here's what never changed, in any era.

47
00:03:05.862 --> 00:03:07.882
The data model outlives the code.

48
00:03:07.882 --> 00:03:14.862
Money has to add up. Every system depends on things you don't control: a bank, a card network, a partner's system.

49
00:03:14.862 --> 00:03:17.740
And somebody has to understand the layer below.

50
00:03:18.100 --> 00:03:20.638
Here's the path. Today, the four eras.

51
00:03:20.638 --> 00:03:25.915
Then, build a whole system AI native, decompose it, and run agents in parallel.

52
00:03:25.915 --> 00:03:29.121
Then governance, quality, skills, and pipelines.

53
00:03:29.121 --> 00:03:32.127
Then, agents that operate enterprise systems.

54
00:03:32.127 --> 00:03:35.600
And last, a capstone: one feature, built end to end.

55
00:03:35.950 --> 00:03:39.383
So, to recap. Architecture bends around what's scarce.

56
00:03:39.383 --> 00:03:42.753
Today, that's knowing the work is right, and context.

57
00:03:42.753 --> 00:03:46.440
So design for checking. And the four constants still hold.

58
00:03:46.790 --> 00:03:49.300
Which gives us rule one, for your agents.

59
00:03:49.300 --> 00:03:52.422
Before you design a system, write down four things.

60
00:03:52.422 --> 00:03:55.422
Where the boundaries are. What the data model is.

61
00:03:55.422 --> 00:03:57.748
What you depend on, and don't control.

62
00:03:57.748 --> 00:03:59.646
And how you'll know it's right.

63
00:03:59.646 --> 00:04:03.380
Then put it in the repository, where every agent can read it.

64
00:04:03.730 --> 00:04:07.238
Now, your turn. Pick the oldest system you still depend on.

65
00:04:07.238 --> 00:04:10.151
When was it built, and what was scarce back then?

66
00:04:10.151 --> 00:04:13.600
Then ask your AI which era's thinking is hiding inside it.

67
00:04:13.950 --> 00:04:20.638
Next: build a whole system, AI native, so agents and people can both read it, and rebuild it.

68
00:04:20.638 --> 00:04:22.220
See you in lesson two.
